    "result_text": "The build is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the disposable-domain blocklist rejection following the project's existing Bean Validation conventions (mirroring `TelephoneValidation`/`PostcodeValidation`).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`rest/validation/EmailDomainValidation.java`** \u2014 a new field-level constraint annotation (validated by `EmailDomainValidator`).\n\n2. **`rest/validation/EmailDomainValidator.java`** \u2014 the validator: extracts the domain after the last `@`, compares it case-insensitively against the blocklist (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`), and rejects a match. A `null` email (optional field) and a value with no domain part are left to the existing `@Email`/`minLength` constraints.\n\n3. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `x-field-extra-annotation` on the `OwnerFields.email` property so the generated `OwnerFieldsDto` carries `@EmailDomainValidation`, which Spring's request-body validation enforces on `POST /api/owners`, yielding **400 Bad Request** (handled by the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice`).\n\nThe acceptance test `errorRejectsDisposableDomain` (posting `x@mailinator.com`) now passes, and the full suite builds green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
