    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the existing `rest/validation` package, which already holds a family of annotation/validator pairs (`Postcode`, `Telephone`, `PetAge`, `RegistrationDate`). The new `EmailDomainValidation`/`EmailDomainValidator` pair follows that established convention precisely:\n\n- **Right unit / right altitude.** A single-field, value-level check on the email string belongs in a field-level `ConstraintValidator<_, String>`, mirroring the telephone validator rather than the class-level `PostcodeValidator` (which legitimately spans two fields). The choice of `@Target(FIELD)` vs the postcode's `@Target(TYPE)` is correct for the scope of this check.\n- **Idiomatic wiring.** It's attached via `x-field-extra-annotation` on the `email` property in `openapi.yml`, exactly how `telephone`, `registrationDate`, and `petAge` are wired \u2014 so it participates in the same Bean Validation flow that produces the 400, with no ad-hoc controller/service code.\n- **No duplication or catch-all.** The blocklist lives as an immutable `Set` local to the validator that owns the check; nothing is bolted onto an unrelated class.\n- **Consistent conventions.** Javadoc tone, the null/absent-domain deference to the `@Email` constraint, and the standard `message`/`groups`/`payload` members all match the sibling validators.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find this entirely natural.",
